From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] Migrate 0416-mesh-02-next-step.patch

Source: 0416-mesh-02-next-step.patch
Apply to the core after the frontend's 0001-0410 series. Generated with
OpenAI Codex; local draft, no human sign-off or upstream submission.

EDEN_NX2_MESH_AS_COMPUTE=1 is the only enable switch; it remains off by
default. No new environment setting, allocation, or GPU wait is added.

ConfigureImpl reads the graphics pipeline handle, then ConfigureDraw calls
RecordMeshComputePasses, which reads mesh_pipeline and task_pipeline before
the scheduler's existing build wait. The builder may still be writing those
non-atomic handles. Making mesh_pipeline last in 0302 does not publish them
across threads. An unfinished build can also make the first draw disappear.

Wait under the existing build mutex at the start of ConfigureImpl for this
path only. Both success and the handled Vulkan failure signal is_built under
that mutex. Unlocking after the wait publishes all handles before the first
CPU read; the existing null checks still reject a failed build. Ordinary
graphics pipelines keep their current asynchronous path. A driver compiler
that never returns can still block this CPU wait.

Checked in a private copy: core16 plus 0182-0410 applies; clang++ -std=c++20
-fsyntax-only on the full changed translation unit passes using local Vulkan,
Boost, Sirit and VMA headers (one existing nodiscard warning in macro.h).
A host test of the extracted wait block passes delayed success, delayed
failure, already-finished and disabled cases. git apply --check passes.
No PS5 build or GPU draw was tested. This does not fix the separate rescaling
push-block failure, output omissions or capped task work documented in
mesh-real-plan.md.
